From 917695a0120fc27a1243db538f98d491de8f96dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leonardo Tada Antunes <leonardotada@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 17:36:22 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] Prerequisites for a concurrent mark: dirty-page tracker,
 scanner rounds, epochs

- d.gc.dirty: which pages of the scannable heap were written since we last
  looked, from the kernel (userfaultfd WP_ASYNC + PAGEMAP_SCAN, Linux 6.7+),
  with a unit test. plans/spikes/dirty measures it. Not used yet.
- Scanner.markRound and addDirtyRange: one round of marking run by a GC thread
  that is not a mutator, and the expansion of a written page into the slots
  that need rescanning (allocated and marked, or born after the mark started).
- The mark skips extents born since it started. They have no marks and count as
  live. The tag is now an epoch that never repeats, not the 8 bit cycle: a
  stale tag that matched would make the mark skip a live object. Blocks born
  during a collection are skipped by the sweep passes (bornEpoch).
- scanWorldPausedData tolerates a caller that is not a druntime thread.
